# Documents this formatter must not touch.
#
# TWO CRITERIA. A document is protected if EITHER holds:
#
#   1. It is contractual or supplied. Reformatting a contractual document by
#      tool is how a wording change gets made by accident and nobody notices
#      in the diff. These are stored exactly as supplied.
#
#   2. Something PARSES it and would break silently on a reflow. A reflow that
#      is cosmetic in prose is a change in a table row or a numbered list that
#      a reader counts. Added 2026-09-11 after the formatter had twice changed
#      a document something depended on: it re-padded the CONVENTIONS tables
#      until the drift test stopped matching, and it folded two newly added
#      open items into the item above them, so they rendered inside another
#      entry's prose and were not items at all. Neither went red.
#      docs/PROJECT-STATE.md, "The formatter has silently changed a document
#      twice".
#
# Criterion 2 is why this list exists as a rule rather than as whoever
# noticed. The next addition is decided against the two criteria above.
#
# WHERE EACH PROTECTED DOCUMENT SITS, AND WHY:
#
#   CLAUDE.md                      criterion 1 — the law; read by every session
#   docs/scope-and-acceptance.md   criterion 1 — derives from the signed
#                                  Inception Report, section 5
#   docs/data-model.md             criterion 1 — the programme manager's
#   docs/data-model-extension.md   criterion 1 — the programme manager's
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT PROTECTED, and the reasoning, so it is not re-argued:
#
#   docs/api/CONVENTIONS.md        Two tests parse it, so criterion 2 applies
#                                  on its face. Left formatted by the owner's
#                                  decision, 2026-09-11: the drift test now
#                                  parses table CELLS rather than lines, which
#                                  absorbs the exposure, and this is the most
#                                  hand-edited document in the project, where
#                                  consistent formatting is worth most. The
#                                  fix was the criterion, not the file. If a
#                                  future test reads it by line again, that
#                                  test is wrong, not this decision.
#   docs/PROJECT-STATE.md          Read structurally by sessions — numbered
#   docs/DECISIONS.md              lists, board rows, sections — but by a
#   docs/HANDOFF.md                reader that can absorb a reflow, unlike a
#   docs/UNITS.md                  test. The second formatter instance shows
#                                  that absorption has a limit; if it bites
#                                  again, criterion 2 applies and they come in.
#   docs/RUNBOOK-restore.md        Read by a CORWADO administrator by numbered
#                                  step. Same reasoning as the four above.
CLAUDE.md
